    "setup_id": "05601a00c6bc4954635863826740b7b61d18798ad2f99e32a9fef415a652990f",
    "text": "To find the integer \\( n \\) such that \\( -90 \\le n \\le 90 \\) and \\(\\sin n^\\circ = \\sin 419^\\circ\\), we need to use the periodic and symmetric properties of the sine function. The sine function is periodic with a period of \\(360^\\circ\\), meaning \\(\\sin(\\theta + 360^\\circ k) = \\sin \\theta\\) for any integer \\(k\\). Additionally, the sine function is symmetric about \\(180^\\circ\\), so \\(\\sin(\\theta) = \\sin(180^\\circ - \\theta)\\).\n\nFirst, we reduce \\(419^\\circ\\) to an angle between \\(0^\\circ\\) and \\(360^\\circ\\):\n\\[419^\\circ = 420^\\circ - 1^\\circ = 360^\\circ + 60^\\circ - 1^\\circ = 60^\\circ - 1^\\circ.\\]\nSince \\(\\sin(419^\\circ) = \\sin(60^\\circ - 1^\\circ)\\), we use the sine subtraction formula:\n\\[\\sin(60^\\circ - 1^\\circ) = \\sin 60^\\circ \\cos 1^\\circ - \\cos 60^\\circ \\sin 1^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cos 1^\\circ - \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 1^\\circ.\\]\nHowever, we don't need to simplify this further to find \\(n\\). Instead, we use the fact that \\(\\sin 419^\\circ = \\sin(360^\\circ + 59^\\circ) = \\sin 59^\\circ\\). Now we need to find an angle \\(n\\) in the range \\(-90^\\circ \\le n \\le 90^\\circ\\) such that \\(\\sin n^\\circ = \\sin 59^\\circ\\). Since \\(\\sin 59^\\circ = \\sin(180^\\circ - 59^\\circ) = \\sin 121^\\circ\\) and \\(\\sin 121^\\circ = \\sin(180^\\circ - 59^\\circ) = \\sin 59^\\circ\\), we can use the fact that \\(\\sin n^\\circ = \\sin 59^\\circ\\) implies \\(n = 59\\) or \\(n = -59\\). Since \\(59\\) is already within the range \\(-90 \\le n \\le 90\\), we have:\n\\[\nn = 59.\n\\]\nThus, the integer \\(n\\) is \\(\\boxed{59}\\).",
    "timing_scope": "native prompt render, tokenize, transfer, CUDA-synchronized generate, decode; excludes loading/scoring",
